nodecode-cartograph is a rewrite of anthony-maio's anthony-maio/cartograph:

  https://github.com/anthony-maio/cartograph
  commit e2e06db1c79f3de60760d9045ae6908ddf4e4b21
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Anthony Maio (LICENSE)

That repository is a TypeScript program -- a CLI, an MCP server and install
adapters -- and its assets/hermes folder is the Hermes plugin: a thin Python
wrapper that runs the installed Node CLI as a subprocess and hands the JSON it
prints to the model as two tools, cartograph_analyze and cartograph_context.

cell.lisp rewrites those two tools so that a Nodecode session answers them
with no external program at all: the walk of the repository, the reading of
each file's import forms, the resolution of a specifier to a file of the same
repository, the ranking over the resulting edges, and the selection of
excerpts for a task are the cell's own Lisp. The tools keep their names and
their arguments (repo, top, and task for the context tool), the answers carry
what the plugin's answers carried (the file count, the edge count, the ranked
files, the dependency hubs, and per-file excerpts with a line range and a
reason), and the section keeps the plugin's own settings (allowed_roots, the
output and excerpt ceilings), with max_files and ignore added to bound the
walk.

Left out, because they belong to the Node program rather than to the plugin:
the LLM-backed wiki and prepare-a-packet commands, the MCP server, the
markdown renderings, the artifact cache, and the install/doctor subcommands.
The plugin's timeout and its subprocess plumbing have no counterpart: nothing
is spawned, so there is no process to time out. Its import reading covers
twenty-odd languages by extension here, where the CLI's own analyzer did its
own set; an edge the reading misses is a miss, never an invention.
